---
title: "When you are on your own but not alone: Learning on ViBe"
author: Aastha Kashyap
vinternship_id: AasthaKashyap_B_kashyapaastha2125@gmail.com
---

When I started my Vinternship journey, I was both nervous and curious. I had never tried web development before — I didn’t know the basics, the terminology, or even where to begin. Everything felt new, and honestly, a little intimidating.

But what surprised me most wasn’t the technology.  
It was the way learning happened.

There were no constant reminders, no fixed classroom, no one pushing me to complete tasks. Just milestones, HP, and the next step waiting to be unlocked. It sounded simple, but that freedom came with its own doubts. I kept wondering if I was moving too slowly or whether I was doing things the right way.

The AI-proctored sessions were another surprise. At first, they felt a bit annoying. Even slight background noise could interrupt the flow and make me rewatch parts of the video. But over time, I noticed something — I was actually paying more attention. The pop-up quizzes made sure I wasn’t just watching; I was understanding.

Then came Discord — something I didn’t expect to matter this much. At first, I mostly observed. People were asking doubts, sharing small wins, and helping each other out. I wasn’t sure if I should jump in.

But whenever I got stuck and finally asked something, someone was always there to respond. Sometimes it was a hint, sometimes a full explanation, and sometimes just reassurance that I wasn’t the only one confused. It was surprising how people you don’t even know can help you walk through difficult parts.

There were also moments when I kept rechecking my understanding — asking questions, trying different approaches, even confirming things through the LLM. And when it finally responded with “yes, that’s right,” it felt like a small but meaningful win. That’s exactly what Visakha did too — guiding, confirming, and helping connect the dots when things felt unclear.

Somewhere along the way, it stopped feeling like just another course. It became an experience — starting with uncertainty, learning at my own pace, and realizing that even when you begin alone, there’s always a community helping you move forward.

A heartfelt thanks to Sudarshan Iyengar sir for promoting learning on such an interesting platform and making it accessible, and Nandan Prabhudesai sir for explaining concepts so simply.  
Thanks to Nitin Sankar sir, Rohit Sharma sir, Jinal Gupta ma'am, Riya Mehta ma'am, Minakshi V ma'am, and the entire VLED team, including the volunteers solving queries on Discord
